<!--
    The Go stack-growth prologue (docs/multilang.md W2/W5), ported as kuna
    `option gomorestack` (P2 flow-classification, default AUTO).

    Every non-leaf function the Go compiler emits opens with a guard: compare
    SP against the goroutine's stackguard (0x10(%r14) under the register ABI),
    and on failure call runtime.morestack_noctxt and LOOP BACK TO THE FUNCTION
    ENTRY to re-run the prologue on the grown stack.  The check is pure runtime
    machinery, but the back-edge makes the entry a loop head: every live
    argument register joins its incoming value with its after-growth value, so
    parameter recovery sees phi-fed arguments on every non-leaf Go function,
    and the body renders wrapped in a loop with a morestack call in its latch.

    Stage model: P2 flow-classification; the shared entry-guard-loop shape
    (`infra/idiom.rs::match_entry_guard_loop`) with the cold-arm callee held to
    `runtime.morestack*`, cut with the `securitycheck`/`stackguard` primitives
    (`Funcdata::removeBranch` + `removeUnreachableBlocks`) in the PRE-SSA
    window (`cleanupcode`'s), so the entry phi never forms and the guard's
    compare and stackguard load die to ordinary dead-code elimination.

    The bytes are a hand-assembled x86-64 `func add(a, b int) int` exactly as
    the Go compiler lays its prologue out:

        100000  cmp  0x10(%r14),%rsp    ; SP vs g.stackguard0
        100004  jbe  100010             ; cannot hold the frame -> grow
        100006  lea  (%rdi,%rsi,1),%rax ; body: a + b
        10000a  ret
        100010  call 101000             ; runtime.morestack_noctxt
        100015  jmp  100000             ; re-run the entry on the grown stack

    P0 assertion: `option gomorestack off|auto|always`.  `auto` (the default)
    acts only on an image whose detected source language is Go; the analyzer
    tier never runs on this bytechunk bootstrap, so the test forces `always`,
    exactly as `rustabi` stage tests force their language-blind mode.
    Pass 1 (option gomorestack off): the bug - the guard renders as a `while`
      loop whose latch calls morestack (assertions #1, #2).
    Pass 2 (option gomorestack always): the fix - the loop and the call are
      gone; both counts stay at exactly one occurrence because the second pass
      contributes none, while the real body (assertion #3) and its `return`
      (assertion #4) appear once per pass.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
493b6610760a488d0437c3cccccccccce8eb0f0000ebe9
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="add"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x101000" name="runtime.morestack_noctxt"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option gomorestack off</com>
  <com>lo fu add</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option gomorestack always</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="GOMORESTACK #1: the growth call renders under off and folds under always" min="1" max="1">morestack_noctxt\(</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GOMORESTACK #2: the entry guard loops under off and folds under always" min="1" max="1">while</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GOMORESTACK #3: the real body survives both passes" min="2" max="2">a0 \+ a1</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GOMORESTACK #4: one return per pass, never a second arm" min="2" max="2">return</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
